  "query_preview": "/Monday through Thursday/... \nStrong, expansive ridging will remain nearly in place next week \nacross the southwest and central United States with the anti-cyclone \ncenter rotating back from central Colorado into New Mexico and \nArizona. Early in the week, sufficient easterly flow will prevail \nover the region juxtaposed with marginally supportive mixing ratios \nof 9-11 g/kg for scattered storms. There remains considerable \nuncertainty regarding a more preferred time frame for higher POPS in \nany given location with mesoscale details likely becoming more \nimportant towards convective evolution. Unfortunately, the mandated \nNBM/WPC forecast during this time only further complicates and \ndiminishes the forecast confidence with numerous instances of \nspurious QPF forcing procedural, un-scientific higher POPs spread \nover uncommon time periods based on climatology and the \npreponderance of trusted model output. In reality, this appears to \nbe a very typical monsoon pattern with alternating convectively \nactive and inactive days. By the latter half of the week, there are \na handful of NAEFS members indicating notable strengthening of the \nhigh pressure with 600dm H5 heights materializing over northern \nAZ/southern UT. It appears the NBM/WPC forecasts are latching onto \nthis scenario as POPs dwindle to much lower than expected values \ngiven the ensemble mean synoptic pattern. The larger impact of this \noutcome would be a return to major HeatRisk and extreme heat \nthresholds towards the end of the week.",
